  "advances": "The proposed universal dyadic weighted selection inequality is false. An exact complete convex downset in dimension ten has a strictly negative selector margin. The proof gives formulas for every center, the complete distribution of the pairs of counts, and exact all-center sums. This rejects this explicit selector. The same census shows that weighting by the full corner count M satisfies the corresponding second-moment inequality on this seed. The example also satisfies the optimized corner bound, as witnessed by one explicitly identified center, and supplies no counterexample to the locked weak Oertel conjecture.\n",
  "author": "research-agent",
  "candidate_id": "62d504cfa822328f",
  "candidate_schema": 2,
  "created_at": "2026-09-09T14:58:31.105897+00:00",
  "depends_on": [],
  "external_sources": "None. Convexity, completeness, the exact difference count, the Boolean block count at every contributing depth, and the full center census are proved directly. No accepted predecessor or unverified prior strategy is used as a premise.\n",
  "kind": "counterexample",
  "proof": "The set \\(P\\) is the simplex with vertices \\(0,20e_1,\\ldots,20e_{10}\\).\nIt is compact, convex, full-dimensional, and contained in the nonnegative orthant. Its complete integer intersection is exactly the displayed \\(D\\).\nIf \\(0\\le z\\le j\\in D\\) are integer vectors, then\n\\(\\sum_i z_i\\le\\sum_i j_i\\le20\\), so \\(z\\in D\\).\nThis verifies the downset hypothesis and includes every boundary lattice point.\nStars and bars, with a nonnegative slack coordinate, gives\n\\(|D|=\\binom{30}{10}=30045015\\).\n\nWe first derive counts that apply to every center. Write\n\\(s=\\sum_i j_i\\), \\(t=20-s\\), and let\n\\(\\lambda=(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_r)\\) be the positive coordinates of \\(j\\), listed in nondecreasing order. Thus \\(0\\le r\\le10\\),\n\\(1\\le\\lambda_1\\le\\cdots\\le\\lambda_r\\), and\n\\(\\sum_i\\lambda_i=s\\le20\\).\nThe empty list occurs exactly at \\(j=0\\).\nAll zero coordinates of \\(j\\) force the corresponding coordinates of every forward displacement and every Boolean vector to be zero. Deleting these deterministic zero coordinates preserves \\(M_j\\) and \\(\\gamma_j\\). In the formulas below, write \\(M(\\lambda)=M_j\\) and \\(\\gamma(\\lambda)=\\gamma_j\\) for any permutation of this center. The dimension in the proposed selector remains ten.\n\nIn these coordinates the original corner set is exactly\n\\[\nA_j=\\left\\{u\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{r}:\nu_i\\le\\lambda_i\\ \\text{for all }i,\\\n\\sum_i u_i\\le t\\right\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nIt is a downset. For an integer vector \\(v\\), let \\(v^+\\) and \\(v^-\\) be its coordinatewise positive and negative parts. Then\n\\(v\\in A_j-A_j\\) if and only if \\(v^+,v^-\\in A_j\\).\nIndeed, if \\(v=a-b\\), then \\(v^+\\le a\\) and \\(v^-\\le b\\), and downward closure applies. Conversely, \\(v=v^+-v^-\\).\nThe two parts have disjoint supports and are uniquely determined by \\(v\\).\nThis gives a bijection, so the subsequent count is of distinct differences.\n\nFor \\(I\\subseteq[r]\\), let \\(F_\\lambda(I;t)\\) count the assignments of positive integers \\(a_i\\) to \\(i\\in I\\) with\n\\(a_i\\le\\lambda_i\\) and \\(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i\\le t\\).\nSet \\(\\binom{b}{a}=0\\) unless \\(b\\ge a\\ge0\\), and \\(\\binom{b}{0}=1\\) for \\(b\\ge0\\).\nInclusion-exclusion after replacing \\(a_i\\) by \\(a_i-1\\) gives the exact formula\n\\[\nF_\\lambda(I;t)\n=\\sum_{T\\subseteq I}(-1)^{|T|}\n\\binom{\\,t-\\sum_{i\\in T}\\lambda_i\\,}{|I|}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nTo check (2), without upper bounds the nonnegative variables \\(a_i-1\\) have sum at most \\(t-|I|\\), hence there are \\(\\binom{t}{|I|}\\) assignments. A violation in coordinate \\(i\\) means \\(a_i-1\\ge\\lambda_i\\); shifting by \\(\\lambda_i\\) in each selected violating coordinate gives exactly the corresponding binomial term. The convention handles an impossible shifted sum. In particular \\(F_\\lambda(\\varnothing;t)=1\\), including \\(t=0\\).\n\nChoose the support \\(I\\) of \\(v^+\\) and the support \\(J\\) of \\(v^-\\). They are disjoint, and the two sum constraints in (1) are separate. The difference count is therefore\n\\[\nM(\\lambda)\n=\\sum_{I\\subseteq[r]}\n\\ \\sum_{J\\subseteq[r]\\setminus I}\nF_\\lambda(I;t)\\,F_\\lambda(J;t).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThis includes zero once and both orientations of every nonzero difference.\n\nHere is an independent direct formula for the weight, including every dyadic depth. For integers \\(a,R\\ge0\\), let\n\\[\nQ(a,R)\n=\\sum_{h=0}^{\\min(a,2R)}\\binom ah\n\\sum_{b=\\max(0,h-R)}^{\\min(h,R)}\\binom hb.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nIt counts ordered disjoint pairs of subsets of an \\(a\\)-element set, each subset having size at most \\(R\\). Namely, choose their union of size \\(h\\), and then choose the first subset of size \\(b\\) inside that union; the second subset is its complement in the union. The restrictions on \\(b\\) are precisely \\(b\\le R\\) and \\(h-b\\le R\\). This accounts for every pair once and gives \\(Q(a,0)=1\\).\n\nFor a scale \\(q\\in\\{1,2,4,8\\}\\), define\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal Y_{\\lambda,q}\n&=\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{r}:\n2qy_i\\le\\lambda_i\\ \\text{for all }i,\\\n2q\\sum_i y_i\\le t\\right\\},\\\\\na_{\\lambda,q}(y)\n&=\\left|\\{i\\in[r]:2qy_i+q\\le\\lambda_i\\}\\right|,\\\\\nR_{\\lambda,q}(y)\n&=\\left\\lfloor t/q\\right\\rfloor-2\\sum_i y_i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nFor \\(q=2^k\\), (1) shows that\n\\(E_{j,k}=\\{u\\ge0:u_i\\le\\lfloor\\lambda_i/q\\rfloor,\\\n\\sum_i u_i\\le\\lfloor t/q\\rfloor\\}\\).\nA nonempty Boolean block has \\(y\\in\\mathcal Y_{\\lambda,q}\\). This is an equivalence: any nonempty block contains its zero Boolean vector, and the inequalities in (5) are exactly the conditions for that zero vector. For such a \\(y\\), an allowed Boolean vector can use exactly the\n\\(a_{\\lambda,q}(y)\\) coordinates identified in (5), and has cardinality at most \\(R_{\\lambda,q}(y)\\). Thus its ordered disjoint-pair count is exactly\n\\(Q(a_{\\lambda,q}(y),R_{\\lambda,q}(y))\\), by (4).\n\nEvery \\(u\\in A_j\\) has \\(u_i\\le10\\), because\n\\(2u_i\\le j_i+u_i\\le s+\\sum_i u_i\\le20\\).\nIt follows that \\(E_{j,k}=\\{0\\}\\) for every \\(k\\ge4\\).\nEach such depth has one nonempty Boolean block, whose disjoint-pair count is one, so the entire tail contributes zero. There are no omitted deeper terms. Consequently\n\\[\n\\gamma(\\lambda)\n=1+\\sum_{q\\in\\{1,2,4,8\\}}\n\\ \\sum_{y\\in\\mathcal Y_{\\lambda,q}}\n\\bigl(Q(a_{\\lambda,q}(y),R_{\\lambda,q}(y))-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThis is the weight in the question, with its constant term and its subtraction of the zero ordered pair in every block unchanged. Formula (6) does not rely on any unverified injection or previous packet.\n\nFor completeness, the following describes the entire center census through explicit finite sums. If \\(m_a\\) is the number of occurrences of \\(a\\) in \\(\\lambda\\), then its multiplicity among the original centers is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{mult}(\\lambda)\n=\\frac{10!}{(10-r)!\\prod_{a=1}^{20}m_a!}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nEvery center occurs in exactly one such permutation class. For each\n\\(s=0,\\ldots,20\\), enumerate all nondecreasing positive lists \\(\\lambda\\) of sum \\(s\\) and length at most ten. Use (2)-(3) for \\(M(\\lambda)\\), (4)-(6) for \\(\\gamma(\\lambda)\\), and (7) for its multiplicity. This specifies both counts for every center, without replacing a corner by another set or counting a difference representation twice.\n\nHere are the resulting sums grouped by \\(s\\). The center count in row \\(s\\) is independently \\(\\binom{s+9}{9}\\).\nThe four numerical columns in a row are the sums of\n\\(\\operatorname{mult}(\\lambda)\\) times, respectively,\n\\(1,M(\\lambda),\\gamma(\\lambda),\\gamma(\\lambda)M(\\lambda)\\)\nover exactly the lists just specified.\n\n| s | Centers | Sum of M | Sum of gamma | Sum of gamma M |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 1 | 10 | 30 | 30 | 90 |\n| 2 | 55 | 455 | 455 | 3895 |\n| 3 | 220 | 4660 | 4480 | 105520 |\n| 4 | 715 | 36315 | 33075 | 2037555 |\n| 5 | 2002 | 230006 | 196466 | 30067798 |\n| 6 | 5005 | 1234765 | 983785 | 354851065 |\n| 7 | 11440 | 5784720 | 4286760 | 3461300280 |\n| 8 | 24310 | 24162230 | 16640090 | 28632543850 |\n| 9 | 48620 | 91467460 | 58568140 | 205126466020 |\n| 10 | 92378 | 317894394 | 189516894 | 1295164632366 |\n| 11 | 167960 | 1021915320 | 566671360 | 7277314972720 |\n| 12 | 293930 | 2998223130 | 1509317490 | 34911519709970 |\n| 13 | 497420 | 7678463100 | 3319499100 | 125459860272300 |\n| 14 | 817190 | 15899945970 | 5724167430 | 284431354425650 |\n| 15 | 1307504 | 23566608520 | 7584561400 | 340814124120296 |\n| 16 | 2042975 | 22131620595 | 7541439615 | 179952047274375 |\n| 17 | 3124550 | 12000216650 | 5395125050 | 38330089553270 |\n| 18 | 4686825 | 3344109725 | 2520382205 | 2508294379065 |\n| 19 | 6906900 | 381852900 | 381852900 | 23238561060 |\n| 20 | 10015005 | 10015005 | 10015005 | 10015005 |\n| Total | 30045015 | 89473785951 | 34823261731 | 1,015,240,625,292,151 |\n\nThe dyadic contributions provide a further exact breakdown.\nFor each listed \\(k\\), the second column is\n\\(\\sum_{j\\in D}\\sum_{y:B_{j,k,y}\\ne\\varnothing}\n(\\operatorname{disj}(B_{j,k,y})-1)\\).\nThe third column multiplies each summand by the full original \\(M_j\\).\nAll \\(k\\ge4\\) have zero in both columns.\n\n| k | Sum of nonzero block-pair counts | Sum of M times those counts |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | 34199907322 | 1,007,334,298,481,270 |\n| 1 | 576327424 | 7670197844640 |\n| 2 | 16961950 | 146645689070 |\n| 3 | 20020 | 9491220 |\n\nAdding \\(|D|\\) to the second-column total gives\n\\(34823261731\\). Adding \\(\\sum_j M_j=89473785951\\) to the third-column total gives \\(1\\,015\\,240\\,625\\,292\\,151\\), as required by the constant term in every \\(\\gamma_j\\).\n\nThe complete distribution of the count pairs is recorded below. Each line gives \\(M_j,\\gamma_j\\), and the number of original centers with exactly those two counts. These 985 lines combine the 2430 permutation classes specified by (7). Their multiplicities sum to \\(30045015\\); multiplying by the second column and by the product of the first two columns gives respectively \\(34823261731\\) and \\(1\\,015\\,240\\,625\\,292\\,151\\).\nIn particular, all \\(10015005\\) centers with \\(s=20\\) have\n\\(A_j=\\{0\\}\\) and \\(M_j=\\gamma_j=1\\). Together with the origin they give the first line's multiplicity \\(10015006\\). This explicitly includes the entire outer boundary.\n\n```text\nM,gamma,number_of_centers\n1,1,10015006\n3,3,20\n5,5,20\n7,7,830\n9,9,65\n11,11,20\n13,11,90\n13,13,18380\n15,13,90\n15,15,20\n17,17,20\n19,15,675\n19,17,90\n19,19,20\n21,19,90\n21,21,171370\n25,21,135\n27,23,90\n27,27,120\n29,23,90\n31,27,90\n31,31,771120\n33,27,360\n33,29,90\n35,29,90\n37,27,540\n37,31,90\n39,29,90\n39,33,90\n43,31,5040\n43,37,90\n43,43,1799280\n45,35,90\n45,37,360\n45,39,90\n49,37,90\n49,41,135\n51,35,90\n51,41,360\n51,43,90\n55,37,10920\n55,43,90\n55,47,90\n57,49,90