1,840\n91803,49635,720\n92221,17699,360\n92753,63461,10\n94087,38701,840\n95553,40641,840\n95645,62475,360\n98375,78693,10\n100067,41397,1260\n100191,23267,1260\n102753,35039,5040\n110257,47353,2520\n112485,38577,7560\n115107,58243,2520\n115425,42139,1260\n122915,55939,2520\n130911,70355,90\n132585,42945,210\n134619,38519,6300\n135239,93041,10\n135739,115735,10\n138663,53667,1260\n148453,53839,5040\n149615,76897,90\n155493,115455,10\n161083,38551,840\n162105,103133,45\n179649,77311,360\n179697,51787,1260\n196263,83799,360\n196819,107693,90\n207773,133471,90\n220043,127175,45\n234823,84289,840\n250527,122473,120\n265935,122571,360\n280747,84857,252\n322369,118029,210\n```\n\nThe finite arithmetic in both census groupings can be audited with the following standalone implementation of (2)-(7). It uses integer arithmetic throughout. In the computation of (3), the array named Z is the subset sum\n\\(Z(B)=\\sum_{J\\subseteq B}F_\\lambda(J;t)\\), so the final expression\n\\(\\sum_I F_\\lambda(I;t)Z([r]\\setminus I)\\) is exactly (3).\nThe partition generator enumerates each list in (7) once.\nEvery loop is finite, and the asserts check the claimed census totals and the strict margin.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom itertools import product\nfrom math import comb, factorial\n\ndef partitions(s, least=1, slots=10):\n    if s == 0:\n        yield ()\n    elif slots:\n        for a in range(least, s + 1):\n            for tail in partitions(s - a, a, slots - 1):\n                yield (a,) + tail\n\ndef choose(a, b):\n    return comb(a, b) if a >= b >= 0 else 0\n\n@lru_cache(None)\ndef Q(r, R):\n    return sum(comb(r, h) * sum(\n        comb(h, a) for a in range(max(0, h - R), min(h, R) + 1))\n        for h in range(min(r, 2 * R) + 1))\n\ndef local_counts(lam, t):\n    r = len(lam)\n    full = (1 << r) - 1\n    subset_sums = [sum(lam[i] for i in range(r) if b >> i & 1)\n                   for b in range(1 << r)]\n    F = []\n    for b in range(1 << r):\n        c, value = b, 0\n        while True:\n            value += (-1) ** c.bit_count() * choose(\n                t - subset_sums[c], b.bit_count())\n            if c == 0:\n                break\n            c = (c - 1) & b\n        F.append(value)\n    Z = F.copy()\n    for i in range(r):\n        for b in range(1 << r):\n            if b >> i & 1:\n                Z[b] += Z[b ^ (1 << i)]\n    M = sum(F[b] * Z[full ^ b] for b in range(1 << r))\n    increments = []\n    for q in (1, 2, 4, 8):\n        T, value = t // q, 0\n        caps = [a // q for a in lam]\n        for y in product(*(range(min(a // 2, T // 2) + 1)\n                           for a in caps)):\n            if 2 * sum(y) <= T:\n                eligible = sum(2 * b < a for a, b in zip(caps, y))\n                value += Q(eligible, T - 2 * sum(y)) - 1\n        increments.append(value)\n    return M, 1 + sum(increments), increments\n\nrows, histogram = [], Counter()\ndepth_counts, depth_products = [0] * 4, [0] * 4\nfor s in range(21):\n    row = [s, 0, 0, 0, 0]\n    for lam in partitions(s):\n        multiplicity = factorial(10) // factorial(10 - len(lam))\n        for c in Counter(lam).values():\n            multiplicity //= factorial(c)\n        M, gamma, increments = local_counts(lam, 20 - s)\n        for i, value in enumerate((1, M, gamma, gamma * M), 1):\n            row[i] += multiplicity * value\n        histogram[M, gamma] += multiplicity\n        for k, value in enumerate(increments):\n            depth_counts[k] += multiplicity * value\n            depth_products[k] += multiplicity * value * M\n    rows.append(row)\ntotals = [sum(row[i] for row in rows) for i in range(1, 5)]\nassert totals == [30045015, 89473785951, 34823261731, 1_015_240_625_292_151]\nassert totals[0] == comb(30, 10)\nassert depth_counts == [34199907322, 576327424, 16961950, 20020]\nassert depth_products == [1_007_334_298_481_270, 7670197844640,\n                          146645689070, 9491220]\nassert len(histogram) == 985\nassert sum(histogram.values()) == totals[0]\nassert sum(c * g for (m, g), c in histogram.items()) == totals[2]\nassert sum(c * g * m for (m, g), c in histogram.items()) == totals[3]\nassert sum(c * m * m for (m, g), c in histogram.items()) == 2_839_381_073_441_231\nassert 1024 * 2_839_381_073_441_231 - totals[0] * totals[1] == 219284978199236279\nassert totals[0] * totals[2] - 1024 * totals[3] == 6_659_020_757_658_341\nprint(*rows, sep=\"\\n\")\nprint(\"M,gamma,number_of_centers\")\nfor (M, gamma), count in sorted(histogram.items()):\n    print(M, gamma, count, sep=\",\")\n```\n\nThe displayed sums give\n\\(2^{10}\\sum_j\\gamma_jM_j=1039606400299162624\\) and\n\\(|D|\\sum_j\\gamma_j=1046265421056820965\\). Their strict inequality proves the stated failure of the selector.\n\nThe complete pair census also gives\n\\(\\sum_j M_j^2=2\\,839\\,381\\,073\\,441\\,231\\) by multiplying each line's multiplicity by the square of its first entry. Therefore\n\\(2^{10}\\sum_j M_j^2-|D|\\sum_j M_j=219284978199236279>0\\).\nThis computes the full-count size-biased test on the same seed; its universal validity remains undecided.\n\nFinally, take the original center \\(j=(1,\\ldots,1)\\).\nEvery \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{10}\\) satisfies \\(u\\le j\\) and\n\\(\\sum_i(j_i+u_i)\\le20\\), so \\(A_j=\\{0,1\\}^{10}\\).\nIts difference set is \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}^{10}\\), of cardinality\n\\(3^{10}=59049\\). Hence this same complete seed satisfies\n\\(\\max_{j\\in D}M_j\\ge59049>|D|/2^{10}\\).\nThe failure established above concerns the prescribed dyadic weighted average alone.\n",
  "statement": "Let \\(n=10\\), and let\n\\[\nP=\\left\\{x\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}^{10}:\\sum_{i=1}^{10}x_i\\le20\\right\\},\n\\qquad\nD=P\\cap\\mathbb Z^{10}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(j\\in D\\), use precisely\n\\(A_j=\\{u\\in\\mathbb Z^{10}:0\\le u\\le j,\\ j+u\\in D\\}\\) and\n\\(M_j=|A_j-A_j|\\), counting each distinct difference once.\nFor \\(k\\ge0\\) and \\(y\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{10}\\), put\n\\[\nE_{j,k}=\\{u\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{10}:2^k u\\in A_j\\},\n\\qquad\nB_{j,k,y}=\\{\\epsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^{10}:2y+\\epsilon\\in E_{j,k}\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(B\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^{10}\\), let\n\\(\\operatorname{disj}(B)=|\\{(\\epsilon,\\eta)\\in B^2:\n\\epsilon_i\\eta_i=0\\text{ for all }i\\}|\\), with the pair ordered.\nUse the specified weight\n\\[\n\\gamma_j\n=1+\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\\n\\sum_{\\substack{y\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{10}\\\\B_{j,k,y}\\ne\\varnothing}}\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{disj}(B_{j,k,y})-1\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe exact all-center counts are\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|D|&=30045015,\\\\\n\\sum_{j\\in D}M_j&=89473785951,\\\\\n\\sum_{j\\in D}\\gamma_j&=34823261731,\\\\\n\\sum_{j\\in D}\\gamma_jM_j&=1\\,015\\,240\\,625\\,292\\,151.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{10}\\sum_{j\\in D}\\gamma_jM_j\n&=1039606400299162624\\\\\n&<1046265421056820965\n=|D|\\sum_{j\\in D}\\gamma_j.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe right side exceeds the left side by \\(6\\,659\\,020\\,757\\,658\\,341\\).\nFor comparison, the exact second moment is\n\\(\\sum_{j\\in D}M_j^2=2\\,839\\,381\\,073\\,441\\,231\\), and this seed satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{10}\\sum_{j\\in D}M_j^2\n&=2907526219203820544\\\\\n&>2688241241004584265\n=|D|\\sum_{j\\in D}M_j.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNevertheless, the center \\(j=(1,\\ldots,1)\\) has \\(M_j=59049\\), and\n\\(2^{10}M_j=60466176>|D|\\). Thus this seed obeys the optimized corner inequality.\n",
  "subgoal_id": "dyadic-corner-size-biased-selection"
